        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 牙医",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "牙医 — yáyī — \"dentist\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "我的牙医很和气 wǒ de yáyī hěn héqi — My dentist is very nice."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 牙 (yá) — four strokes: a short bar on top; a short stroke down on the left that turns and runs right; a vertical that hooks to the left at the foot; and a long stroke falling to the left. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "牙医 (yáyī) is built from 牙 (yá) 'tooth' and 医 'doctor', both inherited from Middle Chinese, ngae and 'i (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the first 'teeth' and the second 'to heal'."
            }
          ]
        },
